---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { PROMPTS, GUARD, TALK, COMMITMENTS, SHIFTS, MOVES, WORKSHOP_LINKS, COURSE_LINKS } from '../../data/teachers';
import { courseHref } from '../../data/courses';
import { feedbackUrl } from '../../lib/feedback';

// Lesson counts come from the lesson collection, so they can never go stale.
const lessons = await getCollection('lessons', (l) => !l.data.draft);
const perCourse = new Map<string, number>();
for (const l of lessons) perCourse.set(l.data.course, (perCourse.get(l.data.course) ?? 0) + 1);
const courses = COURSE_LINKS.filter((c) => perCourse.has(c.slug));
const n = (slug: string) => perCourse.get(slug) ?? 0;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Resources for Teachers — Biochemistrypedia"
  description="Free biology courses and a practical guide for middle and high school life science teachers, to go with the talk Fast Tools, Slow Thinking: the slides, a printable checklist, prompts, and every number with its source."
>
  <article class="wrap teachers" style="padding-top: var(--ddp-space-12)">
    <header class="prose-col">
      <p class="eyebrow">Resources for teachers</p>
      <h1>Fast tools for us. Slow thinking for them.</h1>
      <p>
        Biochemistrypedia is a set of free biology courses: short readings, films of the
        experiments, simulations you can change, and questions with checked answers. You can use
        any of it with your students. This page goes with my talk &ldquo;Fast Tools, Slow
        Thinking&rdquo; at <em>Teaching Life Sciences in the Age of AI</em> (University of Virginia,
        October&nbsp;24, 2026). It has the slides, a checklist you can print, prompts for your own
        use, and a practical guide.
      </p>
      <nav class="jump" aria-label="On this page">
        <a href="#talk">The talk</a>
        <a href="#landed">Where I&rsquo;ve landed</a>
        <a href="#ask">What I&rsquo;d ask of any AI</a>
        <a href="#going">Where this is going</a>
        <a href="#diy">Do it yourself</a>
        <a href="#courses">Courses</a>
        <a href="#prompts">Prompts</a>
        <a href="#workshop">From the workshop</a>
        <a href="#also-free">Also free</a>
        <a href="#sources">Sources</a>
        <a href="#tell-us">Tell us</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <section class="deck-section" aria-labelledby="talk">
      <div class="prose-col">
        <h2 id="talk">The talk</h2>
        <p>
          &ldquo;Fast Tools, Slow Thinking.&rdquo; Click the slides, then use the arrow keys (or the
          buttons below). The pictures in the deck are AI-generated, and the first slide says so.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="deck-frame">
        <iframe
          id="deck"
          src={TALK.deck}
          title="Slides for the talk Fast Tools, Slow Thinking"
          loading="lazy"
          allow="fullscreen"
          allowfullscreen
        ></iframe>
      </div>
      <div class="deck-bar">
        <button type="button" class="btn alt" id="deck-prev">&larr; Previous slide</button>
        <button type="button" class="btn alt" id="deck-next">Next slide &rarr;</button>
        <a class="btn" href={TALK.deck}>Open full screen</a>
        <a class="btn alt" href={TALK.pdf} download>Download PDF</a>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-col">
        <p class="small">
          In the slides, press <kbd>f</kbd> for full screen. On a phone, turn it sideways and use
          &ldquo;Open full screen&rdquo;. The PDF has the main slides only, as pictures; this page
          carries the same text below. A narrated version will be added after the workshop.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="prose-col">
      <h2 id="landed">Where I&rsquo;ve landed</h2>
      <p>
        I&rsquo;ll say it first, so you can judge the rest against it. Two conclusions, and they are
        one idea.
      </p>
      <div class="pair">
        <section class="side fast" aria-labelledby="landed-fast">
          <h3 id="landed-fast"><span class="tag">Fast tools &middot; for teachers</span></h3>
          <p><strong>Teachers are essential, and they&rsquo;re not going away anytime soon.</strong></p>
          <p>
            Any AI tutor should be under the teacher&rsquo;s control, and every exchange should be
            visible to the teacher. That&rsquo;s for safety. It&rsquo;s also because being able to
            question a student&rsquo;s reasoning is the real promise of this for teachers.
          </p>
          <p>AI tools are for us: to make your lives easier and the students&rsquo; experience better.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="side slow" aria-labelledby="landed-slow">
          <h3 id="landed-slow"><span class="tag">Slow thinking &middot; for students</span></h3>
          <p><strong>Leaning on AI is really quite damaging for students, especially younger ones.</strong></p>
          <p>
            They need to be slow about their thinking so they can take an idea on board. The thinking
            stays slow for them.
          </p>
          <p>Your students already use these tools: 54% of US teens used a chatbot for schoolwork in fall 2025 (Pew).</p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <h2 id="ask">What I&rsquo;d ask of any AI in a classroom</h2>
      <p>
        If someone shows you an AI tool for your classroom, whether it&rsquo;s a vendor, your
        district, or me, these are the five questions I&rsquo;d ask.
      </p>
      <ol class="commit">
        {COMMITMENTS.map((c) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{c.lead}</strong>{c.rest && <>{' '}{c.rest}</>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <p>
        I&rsquo;m still building toward several of these myself. The teacher-visible tutor and the
        check-as-you-go levels are not built yet. The pre-work, the fact-checking and the public
        corrections are.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="btn" href="/teachers/checklist">Printable one-page checklist</a>
      </p>

      <h2 id="going">Where this is going</h2>
      <p>
        Five shifts that I think will look obvious in ten years. For each, <strong>now</strong> is
        what is happening and <strong>next</strong> is where I think it goes. Where the next part is
        not built, I say so.
      </p>
      <ol class="shifts">
        {SHIFTS.map((s) => (
          <li class={`shift ${s.side}`}>
            <h3><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">{s.n}</span> Shift {s.n}: {s.title}</h3>
            <p class="tag-line"><span class="tag">{s.side === 'fast' ? 'Fast tools · for teachers' : 'Slow thinking · for students'}</span></p>
            <dl>
              <div><dt>Now</dt><dd>{s.now}</dd></div>
              <div class="next"><dt>Next</dt><dd>{s.next}</dd></div>
            </dl>
            <p><strong>For a teacher:</strong> {s.teacher}</p>
            <p class="built">{s.built}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      <h3 id="habit">Why we teach to the test, and how to loosen it</h3>
      <blockquote class="goodhart">
        <p>&ldquo;When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.&rdquo;</p>
        <footer>Marilyn Strathern (1997), writing about exams, after Charles Goodhart (1975)</footer>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        High-stakes exams are not new: China ran them for more than a thousand years, until 1905.
        Why does the number win? The answer is cheap to grade, and reasoning is expensive to read.
        So the answer becomes the measure, then the target, and teaching bends toward it. That gap,
        between what is cheap to measure and what we care about, is where it happens. The law bites
        hardest on one narrow, high-stakes number, and a single state test is exactly that. Three
        moves blunt it:
      </p>
      <ol class="moves">
        {MOVES.map((m) => <li><strong>{m.lead}</strong> {m.rest}</li>)}
      </ol>
      <p>
        Honestly: state tests and the MCAT are not going away soon. But if understanding is what we
        look at every day, the test becomes a by-product. That is my hope, not something I have
        proved.
      </p>

      <h2 id="diy">Do it yourself</h2>
      <a class="card-link" href="/teachers/do-it-yourself">
        <span class="card-kicker">A practical guide</span>
        <span class="card-title">Ten things you can try, step by step</span>
        <span class="card-body">
          Pre-work in Google Forms, reading a room in fifteen minutes, opening class with
          students&rsquo; own words, meeting students where they are, checking what AI makes,
          keeping any AI tutor yours, and a privacy checklist. Each one says what it is, what you
          need, the steps, and one thing to watch for.
        </span>
        <span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">Read the guide &rarr;</span>
      </a>

      <h2 id="courses">Courses for your classroom</h2>
      <p>
        All of these are free and need no account. Right now the site has {lessons.length} lessons in
        {perCourse.size} courses. Take what is useful.
      </p>
      <div class="course-cards">
        <a class="card-link" href="/teachers/life-science">
          <span class="card-kicker">Grade 7 &middot; Virginia SOL</span>
          <span class="card-title">Life Science</span>
          <span class="card-body">
            {n('life-science')} lessons that follow LS.1 to LS.11, with a standards map and a depth ladder
            up to high school Biology. Planned and released on October 23, 2026.
          </span>
          <span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">For teachers: what is in it &rarr;</span>
        </a>
        <a class="card-link" href="/teachers/biology">
          <span class="card-kicker">High school &middot; Virginia SOL</span>
          <span class="card-title">Biology</span>
          <span class="card-body">
            {n('high-school-biology')} lessons that follow BIO.1 to BIO.8, with a standards map, a depth ladder and a
            supports switch. Released October 20, 2026.
          </span>
          <span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">For teachers: what is in it &rarr;</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="small">
        Both are first editions, checked against published sources by AI models and now in classroom
        testing; every lesson has a &ldquo;Report an error&rdquo; link.
      </p>
      <h3>All courses</h3>
      <ul class="courselist">
        {courses.map((c) => (
          <li><a href={courseHref(c.slug)}>{c.label}</a> <span class="count">{n(c.slug)} lessons</span></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>The courses after Biology were written for college students, so read ahead before assigning.</p>
      <p>
        <a class="btn alt" href="/teachers/demo">Try the five-minute demo as a student</a>
        <a class="btn alt" href="/teachers/how-its-built">How it&rsquo;s built</a>
      </p>

      <h2 id="prompts">Prompts you can use</h2>
      <p>
        Copy a prompt, fill in the [square brackets], and paste it into whichever AI tool your
        district allows. Each one is built to protect slow thinking: students commit to a reason
        before they see an answer, the AI is told to judge the reasoning rather than the
        correctness, and it is told never to invent a source. Everything it writes is a draft for
        you to check.
      </p>
      <p>
        These prompts are for you. I have deliberately not written one for students to paste into
        their own chatbots: you could not see those exchanges, and seeing every exchange is the
        point.
      </p>
      <p class="privacy" role="note">
        <strong>Privacy first.</strong> Strip names and IDs before pasting anything a student
        wrote, and use only tools your district has approved.
      </p>

      <ol class="prompts">
        {PROMPTS.map((p, i) => (
          <li class="prompt" data-prompt>
            <h3 id={`p-${p.id}`}>{i + 1}. {p.title}</h3>
            <p class="protects"><span>Protects:</span> {p.protects}</p>
            {p.studentText && (
              <p class="warn">Student writing goes in here. Remove every name and ID first.</p>
            )}
            <details>
              <summary>Show the prompt</summary>
              <pre tabindex="0" aria-labelledby={`p-${p.id}`}>{p.body}

{GUARD}</pre>
            </details>
            <button type="button" class="copy" data-copy aria-describedby={`p-${p.id}`}>Copy prompt</button>
            <span class="copied" role="status" aria-live="polite"></span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      <h2 id="workshop">From the workshop</h2>
      <p>Shared by the workshop organizers. Each opens in a new tab.</p>
      <ul class="links">
        {WORKSHOP_LINKS.map((l) => (
          <li><a href={l.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{l.label}<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2 id="also-free">Also free: The Molecule Hunters</h2>
      <p>
        <em>The Molecule Hunters: How Biochemists Decoded the Chemistry of Life</em> was written with
        more than 500 UVA biochemistry students. It has 133 profiles of 190 scientists, every one
        began as an AI deep-research dossier, and the students checked them. It is free as a PDF, an
        EPUB and an audiobook. Errors found after publication go on a live fact-check page, each with
        what the book says, what the record shows, and the source.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="btn alt" href="https://deppmannlab.com/book" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The book<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
        <a class="btn alt" href="https://deppmannlab.com/book/fact-check" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Live fact-check page<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
      </p>

      <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Every number in the talk, with its source and year, grouped by slide. Where a number is my own
        data, from one small graduate class, or comes from a secondary report, the page says so.
      </p>
      <p><a class="btn alt" href="/teachers/sources">See the sources</a></p>

      <h2 id="tell-us">Tell us</h2>
      <p>
        Found a mistake, or want a lesson that is not here? The form is anonymous; no sign-in, and
        email is optional.
      </p>
      <p><a class="btn" href={feedbackUrl('Teachers page')}>Report an error or request a lesson</a></p>
      <p class="small">
        These pages use only facts from the talk, its fact files, or this site&rsquo;s own repository.
        More: <a href="/contact">contact</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <style is:global>
    .teachers .eyebrow { font-family: var(--ddp-font-mono); font-size: var(--ddp-text-xs); letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ddp-color-accent); }
    .teachers .small { font-size: var(--ddp-text-sm); color: var(--ddp-color-muted); }
    .teachers .sr-only { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; padding: 0; margin: -1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0); white-space: nowrap; border: 0; }
    .teachers kbd { font-family: var(--ddp-font-mono); font-size: 0.9em; padding: 0.05em 0.4em; border: 1px solid var(--ddp-color-line); border-radius: 4px; background: var(--ddp-color-surface); }
    .teachers .jump { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--ddp-space-2) var(--ddp-space-4); margin: var(--ddp-space-5) 0; font-size: var(--ddp-text-sm); }
    .teachers .jump a { min-height: 32px; display: inline-flex; align-items: center; }

    /* the embedded deck */
    .teachers .deck-section { margin-top: var(--ddp-space-12); }
    .teachers .deck-section h2 { margin-top: 0; }
    .teachers .deck-frame { position: relative; width: 100%; max-width: 960px; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; background: #111; border-radius: var(--ddp-radius); overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid var(--ddp-color-line); }
    .teachers .deck-frame iframe { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; border: 0; }
    .teachers .deck-bar { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--ddp-space-2) var(--ddp-space-3); margin: var(--ddp-space-3) 0 var(--ddp-space-3); }

    .teachers .copy, .teachers .btn { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; min-height: 44px; padding: 0.4em 1.1em; border-radius: 999px; border: 1px solid var(--ddp-color-accent); background: var(--ddp-color-accent); color: #fff; font: inherit; font-weight: 600; text-decoration: none; cursor: pointer; margin: 0 var(--ddp-space-2) var(--ddp-space-2) 0; }
    .teachers .btn.alt { background: transparent; color: var(--ddp-color-accent); }
    .teachers .copy:hover, .teachers .btn:hover { filter: brightness(1.1); }
    .teachers :focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--ddp-color-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }

    /* the two conclusions */
    .teachers .pair { display: grid; gap: var(--ddp-space-4); grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; margin: var(--ddp-space-5) 0; }
    .teachers .side { padding: var(--ddp-space-4) var(--ddp-space-5); border: 1px solid var(--ddp-color-line); border-top-width: 6px; border-radius: var(--ddp-radius); background: var(--ddp-color-surface); }
    .teachers .side.fast { border-top-color: #c98a16; }
    .teachers .side.slow { border-top-color: var(--ddp-color-accent); }
    .teachers .side h3 { margin: 0 0 var(--ddp-space-3); font-size: var(--ddp-text-base); }
    .teachers .side p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
    .teachers .tag { font-family: var(--ddp-font-mono); font-size: var(--ddp-text-xs); letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; font-weight: 600; }
    .teachers .side.fast .tag, .teachers .shift.fast .tag { color: #8a5a00; }
    .teachers .side.slow .tag, .teachers .shift.slow .tag { color: var(--ddp-color-accent); }
    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { .teachers .side.fast .tag, .teachers .shift.fast .tag { color: #e9b44c; } }

    .teachers ol.commit { padding-left: 1.4em; }
    .teachers ol.commit li { margin-bottom: var(--ddp-space-2); }

    /* shifts */
    .teachers ol.shifts { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: var(--ddp-space-5) 0; display: grid; gap: var(--ddp-space-4); }
    .teachers .shift { padding: var(--ddp-space-4) var(--ddp-space-5); border: 1px solid var(--ddp-color-line); border-left-width: 6px; border-radius: var(--ddp-radius); background: var(--ddp-color-surface); }
    .teachers .shift.fast { border-left-color: #c98a16; }
    .teachers .shift.slow { border-left-color: var(--ddp-color-accent); }
    .teachers .shift h3 { margin: 0 0 var(--ddp-space-1); font-size: var(--ddp-text-lg); }
    .teachers .shift .num { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; width: 1.6em; height: 1.6em; margin-right: 0.3em; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--ddp-color-accent); color: #fff; font-family: var(--ddp-font-mono); font-size: 0.8em; }
    .teachers .shift .tag-line { margin: 0 0 var(--ddp-space-3); }
    .teachers .shift dl { margin: 0 0 var(--ddp-space-3); display: grid; gap: var(--ddp-space-2); }
    .teachers .shift dl > div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 4.5rem 1fr; gap: var(--ddp-space-3); padding: var(--ddp-space-2) var(--ddp-space-3); border-radius: var(--ddp-radius-sm); background: var(--ddp-color-bg); }
    .teachers .shift dl > div.next { border: 1px solid var(--ddp-color-line); }
    .teachers .shift dt { font-family: var(--ddp-font-mono); font-size: var(--ddp-text-xs); letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ddp-color-muted); padding-top: 0.25em; }
    .teachers .shift dd { margin: 0; }
    .teachers .shift .built { margin: 0; font-size: var(--ddp-text-sm); color: var(--ddp-color-muted); font-style: italic; }
    .teachers .shift p { margin-bottom: var(--ddp-space-2); }
    .teachers blockquote.goodhart { margin: var(--ddp-space-4) 0; }
    .teachers blockquote.goodhart p { font-family: var(--ddp-font-serif); font-size: var(--ddp-text-xl); margin-bottom: var(--ddp-space-2); }
    .teachers blockquote.goodhart footer { font-size: var(--ddp-text-sm); color: var(--ddp-color-muted); }
    .teachers ol.moves { padding-left: 1.4em; }
    .teachers ol.moves li { margin-bottom: var(--ddp-space-2); }

    /* link cards */
    .teachers .card-link { display: grid; gap: var(--ddp-space-1); padding: var(--ddp-space-4) var(--ddp-space-5); border: 1px solid var(--ddp-color-line); border-radius: var(--ddp-radius); background: var(--ddp-color-surface); text-decoration: none; color: inherit; margin: var(--ddp-space-4) 0; }
    .teachers .card-link:hover { border-color: var(--ddp-color-accent); box-shadow: var(--ddp-shadow); }
    .teachers .card-kicker { font-family: var(--ddp-font-mono); font-size: var(--ddp-text-xs); letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ddp-color-accent); }
    .teachers .card-title { font-family: var(--ddp-font-serif); font-size: var(--ddp-text-xl); color: var(--ddp-color-ink); }
    .teachers .card-body { color: var(--ddp-color-ink-soft); }
    .teachers .card-go { color: var(--ddp-color-accent); font-weight: 600; margin-top: var(--ddp-space-2); }
    .teachers .course-cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--ddp-space-4); }
    .teachers .course-cards .card-link { margin: var(--ddp-space-2) 0; }
    .teachers .courselist { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(15rem, 1fr)); gap: var(--ddp-space-2) var(--ddp-space-5); list-style: none; padding: 0; }
    .teachers .courselist .count { font-size: var(--ddp-text-sm); color: var(--ddp-color-muted); white-space: nowrap; }
    .teachers ul.links { padding-left: 1.3rem; list-style: disc; }
    .teachers ul.links li { margin-bottom: var(--ddp-space-2); }

    /* prompts */
    .teachers .privacy { padding: var(--ddp-space-3) var(--ddp-space-4); border-left: 3px solid var(--ddp-color-secondary); background: var(--ddp-color-surface); }
    .teachers ol.prompts { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: var(--ddp-space-5) 0; display: grid; gap: var(--ddp-space-4); }
    .teachers .prompt { padding: var(--ddp-space-4) var(--ddp-space-5); border: 1px solid var(--ddp-color-line); border-radius: var(--ddp-radius); background: var(--ddp-color-surface); }
    .teachers .prompt h3 { margin: 0 0 var(--ddp-space-2); font-size: var(--ddp-text-lg); }
    .teachers .protects { margin: 0 0 var(--ddp-space-3); font-size: var(--ddp-text-sm); color: var(--ddp-color-muted); }
    .teachers .protects span { font-family: var(--ddp-font-mono); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em; font-size: var(--ddp-text-xs); color: var(--ddp-color-accent); }
    .teachers .warn { margin: 0 0 var(--ddp-space-3); font-size: var(--ddp-text-sm); color: var(--ddp-color-caution, var(--ddp-color-ink)); }
    .teachers .prompt summary { cursor: pointer; font-size: var(--ddp-text-sm); color: var(--ddp-color-accent); min-height: 32px; }
    .teachers .prompt pre { white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere; margin: var(--ddp-space-3) 0; padding: var(--ddp-space-3) var(--ddp-space-4); background: var(--ddp-color-bg); border: 1px solid var(--ddp-color-line); border-radius: var(--ddp-radius-sm); font-family: var(--ddp-font-mono); font-size: var(--ddp-text-sm); line-height: 1.5; max-height: 26rem; overflow-y: auto; }
    .teachers .copied { margin-left: var(--ddp-space-3); font-size: var(--ddp-text-sm); color: var(--ddp-color-verified); }

    @media (max-width: 720px) {
      .teachers .pair, .teachers .course-cards { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
      .teachers .shift dl > div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--ddp-space-1); }
      .teachers .shift, .teachers .prompt, .teachers .side { padding: var(--ddp-space-4); }
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    // Copy buttons: the prompt text lives in the page, so copying works without any network.
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-prompt]').forEach((card) => {
      const btn = card.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy]');
      const pre = card.querySelector('pre');
      const msg = card.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.copied');
      if (!btn || !pre || !msg) return;
      btn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
        const text = pre.textContent ?? '';
        let ok = false;
        try {
          await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
          ok = true;
        } catch {
          // Fallback for browsers that block the clipboard API: select the text so the
          // reader can copy it by hand.
          const det = card.querySelector('details');
          if (det) det.open = true;
          const r = document.createRange();
          r.selectNodeContents(pre);
          const s = window.getSelection();
          s?.removeAllRanges();
          s?.addRange(r);
        }
        msg.textContent = ok ? 'Copied.' : 'Press Ctrl/Cmd+C to copy the selected text.';
        setTimeout(() => (msg.textContent = ''), 3500);
      });
    });

    // Previous / next buttons for the embedded deck. The deck is on the same origin, so a key
    // event can be handed to it; if that ever fails, the keyboard and a click on the slide still work.
    const deck = document.getElementById('deck') as HTMLIFrameElement | null;
    const press = (key: string) => {
      try {
        const doc = deck?.contentDocument;
        if (!doc) return;
        doc.dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent('keydown', { key, bubbles: true, cancelable: true }));
      } catch { /* ignore */ }
    };
    document.getElementById('deck-prev')?.addEventListener('click', () => press('ArrowLeft'));
    document.getElementById('deck-next')?.addEventListener('click', () => press('ArrowRight'));
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
